Consider a many-fermion quantum system (a nucleus) governed by a microscopic Hamiltonian H acting on the antisymmetric Fock space. A microscopic theory of large-amplitude collective motion (LACM) seeks a low-dimensional collective submanifold M embedded in the full TDHF (time-dependent Hartree–Fock) or TDHFB (Hartree–Fock–Bogoliubov) phase space of Slater determinants (or generalized product states) such that:

(i) M is approximately invariant under the dynamics generated by H;
(ii) the dynamics restricted to M decouples maximally from non-collective intrinsic degrees of freedom;
(iii) M is determined self-consistently from H (e.g., via the self-consistent collective coordinate method, the adiabatic self-consistent collective coordinate method, or equivalent invariance-principle formulations of the TDHF/TDHFB equations), without prescribing collective coordinates by hand.

Existing constructions of such submanifolds and the resulting collective Hamiltonian are formulated in the laboratory frame (or in a frame with vanishing or small angular velocity) and are valid only in the low-spin regime, where the modification of the intrinsic mean field induced by rotation can be neglected.

Problem. Construct a microscopic theory of LACM applicable to high-spin states that satisfies all of the following:

(a) it is formulated in a rotating intrinsic frame so that the angular velocity ω (or, equivalently, the total angular momentum I) appears as a dynamical variable on equal footing with the LACM coordinates;

(b) the collective submanifold M(ω) and the associated collective inertia and potential are determined self-consistently together with the cranked mean field, so that variations of the intrinsic single-particle structure caused by rapid rotation (e.g., Coriolis antipairing, alignment of high-j quasiparticles, shape changes with spin) are included nonperturbatively rather than treated as external perturbations on a low-spin solution;

(c) it reduces, in the limit ω → 0, to a known self-consistent microscopic LACM theory at low spin;

(d) after requantization on M(ω), it yields a collective Schrödinger-type equation describing coupled large-amplitude shape (and pairing) dynamics with rotation, capable of describing phenomena such as band crossings, shape transitions along the yrast line, and rotation-induced changes of the collective potential and mass parameters.

Provide such a construction, or prove that no construction satisfying (a)–(d) exists within the TDHF/TDHFB framework.

